3. Variables by where they are used:

	3.a. Local variables:

		Declared inside a function or a block. They are accessible only within that scope.


	        int main(voiod)
	        {
	            int     i;

	            i = 1;
	            printf("%d\n", i);
	            return (0);
	        }

	3.b. Global variables:

	    Declared outside all functions. They have file scope in the ordinary case and static storage duration.

			int     i;

	        i = 1;

	        int main(void)
	        {
	            printf("%d\n", i);
	            return(0);
    	    }

	3.c. Static variables:

	    The static keyword gives a variable static storage duration.
	    A local static variable retains its value between function calls.

	    Unlike an ordinary local variable, count is 
		initialisedonly once and keeps its value between calls.

	        void counter(void)
	            {
	                static int      count;

	                count = 0;
	                count++;
	                printf("%d\n", count);
	            }

	            int main(void)
	            {
	                counter(); // 1
	                counter(); // 2
	                counter(); // 3
	                return(0);
	            }

	3.d. Automatic variables:

    	Ordinary local variables are automatic by default. Their lifetime generally lasts until execution leaves their block.

	        void example(void)
	            {
	                int     x;

	                x = 12;                 Here x is an automatic variable.
	            }

	3.e. Constants and read-only variables:

	    A const-qualified object cannot be modified through particular qualified access.

	        const int       max_score;

	        max score = 100;

